gdm 48.0: rename a parameter called `bool`, which C23 made a keyword

common/gdm-settings-utils.h:47 declares

    gboolean gdm_settings_parse_value_as_boolean (const char *value,
                                                  gboolean   *bool);

and the definition in gdm-settings-utils.c:290 matches. `bool` was an ordinary
identifier through C17; C23 promoted it to a KEYWORD, and gcc 15 -- which this
tree uses -- defaults to -std=gnu23. So the header no longer parses:

    gdm-settings-utils.h:47:77: error: expected ';', ',' or ')' before 'bool'

and every translation unit that includes it fails, which is why the log also
shows an implicit-declaration error for the function in gdm-settings-direct.c:
that one is a consequence, not a second defect.

Renamed to bool_out in the five places it appears across the two files. A
parameter name is not part of the ABI and no caller changes.

The alternative was -Dc_std=gnu17, or CFLAGS=-std=gnu17 as gmp's pkg.env does.
Rejected: it compiles the whole package to an older standard to work around one
identifier, and it would silently keep doing so after upstream fixes this.

